From 01a5b75382b047a7d38c1a09d4594917ef082cf5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: stephan-xyz <stephan@zehrer.xyz>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 16:18:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/20] xhcid: blocking endpoint reads on worker threads, not
 in the request loop

xhcid served its scheme requests one after the other
(scheme_utils::Blocking). A read on an interrupt endpoint (usbhidd's
transfer_read) ran in line with block_on and returned only when the
device sent a report, and it held the handle table entry while it
waited. Until then every other request waited too.

On a Pi 400 the internal keyboard reports every 4 ms (SET_IDLE), so its
reads return at once. A mouse that refuses SET_IDLE reports only when it
moves: while it lay still its read blocked xhcid completely, the
keyboard's reads and usbhubd's port polls included. While it moved, the
reads took turns: key presses were lost, the mouse got too few reports
and its motion saturated (redox-rpi #592).

- main.rs: its own request loop, like Blocking, except that a read on
  an endpoint data handle other than the default control pipe goes to
  a worker thread, which answers when the transfer completes. Workers
  stay; one more starts when a read comes in while all of them wait.
- scheme.rs: a data read drops the handle table guard before it waits.
  is_blocking_data_handle tells the loop which reads to hand off.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
